I think you're identifying the right optimization target.

Claude's expensive work should be the work that requires Claude's judgment. Everything else is overhead.

If I were redesigning the harness, I'd separate the Canon Gate into explicit phases:

Phase 1 – Intake (cheap)

Read new experimental/ content.

Classify what it touches (rules, lore, items, quests, etc.).

Detect possible conflicts.

If no conflicts are found, proceed automatically.


Phase 2 – Integration (cheap)

Move or merge content.

Update indexes.

Update cross-references.

Propagate renamed concepts.

Log changes.


This is mostly mechanical.

Phase 3 – Arbitration (expensive) Only invoked when:

Canon conflicts.

Authority-level questions.

Rule redefinitions.

Architectural inconsistencies.

Ambiguous placement.


Only here should Claude spend lots of reasoning tokens.


---

The biggest mindset shift is this:

> Default to integrating, not evaluating.



Right now it sounds like Claude still feels responsible for proving every proposal deserves to exist.

But you've already done that evaluation while creating it.

Claude should instead assume:

> "This proposal has creative approval. My job is to determine whether it fits cleanly into the existing architecture."



That's a much narrower—and cheaper—question.

There's another opportunity too. Your harness could explicitly distinguish between:

Semantic work (thinking)

Mechanical work (editing)


Mechanical work is things like:

moving files,

updating links,

fixing references,

changing headings,

propagating names.


Those don't need deep deliberation. If the harness recognizes them as deterministic tasks, it can avoid spending a large reasoning budget on them.

In software terms, you're moving from a pipeline where every commit goes through a full design review to one where:

routine changes merge automatically,

anything structurally risky opens a review,

constitutional changes require you.
